  "query_preview": "..\nAn upper-level trough is digging over the western US. Subsequent \nmoisture-ladden SW flow with aloft will produce precipitation\ntonight through Monday. Embedded minor perturbations within the\nflow may cause localized areas of greater precipitation. Polar \nair associated with the system will cause colder temperatures this\nweekend.\n\nRain totals this weekend have been trending higher compared to\nprevious model runs. At least 0.5\" of rain is expected across the\nCWA. Snow is possible Sunday night, but warm ground temperatures \nwill prevent any accumulating snowfall from sticking, the only exception\nbeing the Little Rockies.\n\nThe trough will eventually split as a cut-off low develops over \nthe Four Corners region, where it will remain quasi-stationary \nthrough Wednesday morning.\n\nModel agreement deteriorates significantly from this point \nonward. There is little confidence in weather conditions Thursday\nand Friday.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \nConfidence is increasing that the precip on Saturday through \nMonday evening will bring more than expected amounts of \nprecipitation, though given warm ground temperatures and marginal \nthermal profiles, below average confidence exists on snow as a \nprecipitation type, timing of any changeover, or any accumulation,\nsave for the higher terrain such as the Little Rockies.\n\nDeviations in QPF are trending higher versus prior model runs.\n\nConfidence is also below average on precipitation chances later in\nthe week as ensembles show increasing spread at larger time\nscales. It is possible the low tracks too far to the southeast for\nanything significant, other than increasing clouds and light\nprecipitation. Will be monitoring in the coming days for any\ntrends.\n\nFIN/DON",
